WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.773
Block nested loop is fine for small-times-big joins.

00:00:05.773 --> 00:00:09.546
When both tables dwarf RAM and the join is an equality, its inner scan goes quadratic.

00:00:09.546 --> 00:00:13.320
Hash partition join turns that exact same join linear.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.093
Here is the trick.

00:00:17.093 --> 00:00:21.254
Phase one, partition.

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:25.415
Take R, the Users table, and hash every row on the join key.

00:00:25.415 --> 00:00:29.576
Each row routes into one of a few partition files on disk, R zero, R one, R two.

00:00:29.576 --> 00:00:33.737
Now do the same to S, the Listens table, with the exact same hash function.

00:00:33.737 --> 00:00:37.898
That is the whole insight.

00:00:37.898 --> 00:00:42.059
Same hash means the same key always lands in the same partition number on both sides.

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:46.220
Mickey hashes to one partition in Users and the matching partition in Listens.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:50.381
Phase two, join.

00:00:50.381 --> 00:00:54.542
For each partition number, load R's piece into RAM, build a hash table on the key, then probe with S's piece.

00:00:54.542 --> 00:00:58.703
Each pair joins independently, all in RAM.

00:00:58.703 --> 00:01:02.864
Add up the matches, eight output rows.

00:01:02.864 --> 00:01:07.025
The cost.

00:01:07.025 --> 00:01:11.186
Read each table, write the partitions, read them back, write the output.

00:01:11.186 --> 00:01:15.347
Three times the quantity N plus M, plus OUT.

00:01:15.347 --> 00:01:19.508
Linear.

00:01:19.508 --> 00:01:22.294
And in code, two passes.

00:01:22.294 --> 00:01:25.081
Partition both tables on the same hash, then join the matching buckets in RAM.

00:01:25.081 --> 00:01:27.867
No rescans.

00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:31.317
Why bother.

00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:34.767
Look at the scale.

00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:38.217
Users at one hundred gigabytes, Listens at eight hundred, RAM at sixty-four.

00:01:38.217 --> 00:01:41.666
Neither side fits.

00:01:41.666 --> 00:01:45.116
Block nested loop would re-scan the larger table once per outer block, far too slow.

00:01:45.116 --> 00:01:48.566
Sort-merge would have to sort both, also expensive.

00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:52.016
When the join is an equality and neither side fits, hash partition join is the answer.

00:01:52.016 --> 00:01:56.761
It chains for more tables.

00:01:56.761 --> 00:02:01.505
Users, Listens, Songs.

00:02:01.505 --> 00:02:06.250
Hash partition the first two on user id.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:10.995
Take that intermediate result, then hash partition it and Songs on song id.

00:02:10.995 --> 00:02:15.739
Each step is the same two-phase pattern, and the cost grows linearly per join, not multiplicatively.

00:02:15.739 --> 00:02:20.484
As we will see later, these underpin most modern data systems, including Postgres, BigQuery, and Spark.

00:02:20.484 --> 00:02:27.125
Hash partition join lets you join tables far larger than RAM by reducing the whole problem to many small in-RAM joins.

00:02:27.125 --> 00:02:33.766
The one real failure mode is skew.

00:02:33.766 --> 00:02:40.407
If user id one is a system account with ten million listens, its partition overflows RAM while the others fit.

00:02:40.407 --> 00:02:47.048
Detect the hot bucket in phase one, re-partition it with a different hash, recurse, and you are fine.

00:02:47.048 --> 00:02:53.689
Next, when the data is already sorted, sort-merge beats hash partition join.
